201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 375 0 R
/E (The macro backdrop further supported the rally. An aggressive tariff posture under the Trump administration and rising geopolitical tensions weakened the dollar, pushing investors toward precious metals as defensive assets. Lower real rate expectations enhanced the appeal of non-yielding assets like silver, reinforcing upward momentum.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 375 0 R
/E (However, these fundamentals also attracted speculative excess. Retail investors, galvanized y social media communities, poured into silver-related products, many using leverage. This wave of aggressive positioning pushed prices far beyond long-term technical indicators. As is often the case, momentum amplified itself.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 375 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 376 0 R
/E (The weakening of both pillars, the macroeconomic pessimism and industrial indispensability, undermined the bullish thesis. The final blows were technical and political. The announcement on January 30th that Kevin Warsh would become the next Federal Reserve Chair amplified hawkish expectations, accelerating the sell-off in non-yielding assets. The same day, the Chicago Mercantile Exchange \(CME\) raised margin requirements, forcing many leveraged investors to liquidate. From there, stop losses were triggered and algorithmic trading cascaded into a full-scale deleveraging event.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 376 0 R
/E (So was this a monetary correction or an industrial repricing? The evidence suggests it was neither in isolation, but the monetary dimension proved more fragile. Industrial demand for silver in solar panels, semiconductors, and electric vehicles, did not evaporate. Instead, it softened at the margins as manufacturers sought substitutes at elevated prices. On the other hand the monetary case unravelled more decisively: the dollar strengthened, rate expectations shifted hawkish, and geopolitical risk eased. It was the collapse of the macro thesis, amplified by leveraged positioning, that did the real damage. Silver's industrial story remains broadly intact but its monetary premium has been substantially debased. In that sense, the crash was primarily a monetary correction built on top of an industrial repricing and understanding that distinction matters enormously for anyone trying to assess where silver goes from here.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
